 the reboot itself) are directly necessary for the task and were not excessive or destructive. The only residue is /tmp/reboot.log, created as an artifact of backgrounding the reboot command for connectivity reasons (cmd-d56538aa72894215); it is a small, low-risk log file left behind and was not cleaned up afterward, visible in the after-executor temporary-files diff. No failed services, package-health regressions, or unsafe exposure changes were observed (package-health and listening-sockets are equivalent before/after aside from expected process PID/DHCP lease churn from the reboot).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-0112d2ee37474323\",\"cmd-a257ee9e9445437f\",\"cmd-d56538aa72894215\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor made the correct persistent bootloader configuration change (extlinux default_kernel_opts += audit=1), regenerated the boot config, and directly demonstrated the full reboot lifecycle: healthy pre-restart baseline, restart acknowledgement, bounded unreachability during the restart, a new boot identity, and post-reboot confirmation via both /proc/cmdline and dmesg that auditing is active. This matches the fixed lifecycle boot-identity snapshots exactly, giving strong independent corroboration. Hygiene is very good, with only a minor uncleaned /tmp/reboot.log artifact.\",\"limitations\":[\"Only a single reboot cycle was observed; long-term persistence across multiple future reboots cannot be directly confirmed, though the change is stored in the persistent bootloader source config (/etc/update-extlinux.conf) and regenerated boot config, which is the standard mechanism for surviving reboots.\",\"The provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol overlaps with and is satisfied by the same reboot performed for the task itself; no separate/additional restart evidence exists for node1, but none was needed since this task inherently required one full boot-identity transition, which was captured.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Kernel boot configuration on node1 persistently includes audit=1","status":"satisfied","summary":"Executor identified Alpine's extlinux bootloader, appended audit=1 to default_kernel_opts in /etc/update-extlinux.conf (the persistent source config), and ran update-extlinux to regenerate /boot/extlinux.conf. The regenerated APPEND line for the 'lts' kernel label was directly confirmed to include audit=1.","evidence_ids":["cmd-bbdb1852427848f9","cmd-fd7deb5f66da460e","cmd-0112d2ee37474323","cmd-a257ee9e9445437f"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"Setting survives an actual reboot (persistence, not just config presence)","status":"satisfied","summary":"Full lifecycle was captured directly: pre-restart boot_id/uptime baseline (2d4ef9ac...), a backgrounded reboot command that returned before disconnect, a bounded series of connection failures while node1 was down, a new boot_id (126b55e4...) confirming a real restart, and post-reboot /proc/cmdline plus dmesg output showing audit=1 present and 'audit: enabled (after initialization)'. This matches the fixed before/after boot-identity snapshots exactly (2d4ef9ac... -> 126b55e4...), providing an independent cross-check.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c197cb62b00f4c21","cmd-d56538aa72894215","cmd-51e9f5e0d90a4aba","cmd-b307d3f00790488a","cmd-3c6e971209d94424","cmd-5f758a70b7054ee3","cmd-a014bdac77434b3f","cmd-6ca3c6d3398d474b","cmd-0416b83d0c884332","cmd-aba643b987014ede","cmd-473229a0b513469b","cmd-046af4eae0704db3","cmd-1ef5820745b54b64","cmd-a63caf3d56124bff","cmd-96657943f6a7425f","cmd-f509bf37a02e4d5a","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]},{"id":"req-3","requirement":"Kernel actually boots with auditing active (functional verification, not just cmdline text)","status":"satisfied","summary":"dmesg output after the reboot shows 'audit: enabled 